//? Title: NICE: Exact Inversion and a Constant Volume Factor
//? Subtitle: Additive couplings, actual Jacobians and a short banana-density fit
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A density model must assign a normalized probability to each observation while
also generating new observations. An invertible map makes both operations
tractable: transform points to a Gaussian base for likelihood, and invert base
draws for sampling. This native C++ study checks a hand-set additive coupling
before fitting four alternating NICE stages to the original procedural banana
pools. The complete program retains all 48 Adam writes, both actual Jacobians,
roundtrips and all 1,024 latent-only samples. Its fixed-final test NLL is
2.994648218 nats per two-coordinate observation, slightly worse than the
fitting-only Gaussian's 2.994631823 and well above analytic truth's 2.796480843.
Exact source reproduction and invertibility checks establish implementation
fidelity; the short fit gives no improved-density benchmark result.
)
\toc

> Turn a simple base density into a nonlinear observation density

[Dinh, Krueger and Bengio, NICE: Non-linear Independent Components Estimation
(2014)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.8516) composes additive couplings with easily
computed inverses and determinants. For a partition into coordinates a,b,

$$ (a,b)\longmapsto(a,b+s(a)),\qquad
J=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\s'(a)&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad \det J=1. $$
\alttext(An additive coupling preserves a, adds a shift depending only on a to b, and has a triangular Jacobian with both diagonal entries one.)

The inverse keeps a and subtracts the same shift. Alternate the conditioning
coordinate to change both coordinates across stages. The compact source has
four shifts, each a 1->16 tanh->1 network, followed by a positive learned global
diagonal scale z=exp(log_scale)×h. Its forward log determinant is the scalar
sum(log_scale), identical for every point. Jacobian **entries** can still vary
with position. With a standard two-dimensional Gaussian base,

$$ \log p_X(x)=-\log(2\pi)-\tfrac12\|z\|^2+
\sum_{c=0}^1\mathrm{log\_scale}_c,\qquad
\mathcal L=-\frac1N\sum_i\log p_X(x_i). $$
\alttext(The observation log density is standard Gaussian latent log density plus the forward log determinant; the training loss is its negative mean over rows.)

Sampling draws z from that base, unscales it and reverses the four shifts.
There is no rejection, ancestral coordinate feedback, numerical ODE solve or
approximate determinant in this model. The program still differentiates both
maps to check the analytic determinant against their **actual** Jacobians.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/nice_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::Diagram d(28,1150);
 dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle s;s.width=10;s.height=5;s.font_size=32;
 d.node("split",{-24,0},"Coordinates a,b\nCondition on a",s)
 .node("shift",{-11,0},"Shift s(a)\n1 -> 16 tanh -> 1",s)
 .node("add",{2,0},"Keep a\nOther b + s(a)",s)
 .node("stack",{15,0},"4 alternating blocks\nPositive global scale",s)
 .node("density",{28,0},"Gaussian base\nAdd forward logdet",s)
 .edge("split","shift").edge("shift","add").edge("add","stack").edge("stack","density")
 .save_svg("build/nice_method.svg");
}
