> Hand-check the banana transform before generating its pools

The source distribution is deliberately synthetic and analytic:
u,v independent standard normals, x0=u, x1=v+.5(u²−1). It is a triangular
map with determinant one and inverse v=x1−.5(x0²−1). Therefore its true log
density is the standard Gaussian log density of that inverse. For v=0 and
u=(-2,-1,0,1,2), the changed coordinates are (1.5,0,-.5,0,1.5).
The density has one curved mode. More generally a smooth invertible map with
constant determinant preserves the base density's critical points: gradients
transform by an invertible Jacobian transpose. A Gaussian base with additive
NICE and only a global scale cannot create multiple isolated modes. This
restriction does not prevent modeling a single curved banana density.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/nice_banana_toy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;namespace m=n::models;auto type=n::DType::Float64;
 auto u=n::from_vector(std::vector<double>{-2,-1,0,1,2},{5});auto base=n::stack({u,n::zeros({5},type)},1);
 auto points=m::BananaPoints(base,.5,type);auto target=n::from_vector(std::vector<double>{1.5,0,-.5,0,1.5},{5});
 if(!n::equal_bits(n::select(points,1,1),target)||n::max(n::abs(m::BananaLogProbability(points)-m::StandardNormalLogProbability(base))).item()>1e-14)throw std::runtime_error("Analytic banana");
 auto axis=n::arange(0,129,1,type)/32.-2.;auto curve=m::BananaPoints(n::stack({axis,n::zeros({129},type)},1),.5,type);
 dm::graphics::RPlot::create().lines(axis.to_vector<double>(),n::select(curve,1,1).to_vector<double>(),"v=0 banana curve",{37,99,235})
 .points(u.to_vector<double>(),std::vector<double>(5,0),"Base v=0",{100,116,139},5)
 .points(u.to_vector<double>(),target.to_vector<double>(),"Five native points",{220,38,38},4)
 .y_limits(-.7,2.6).x_label("u = x0").y_label("x1 = v + .5(u^2 - 1)")
 .title("Unit-volume bending gives an analytic teaching density").save_svg("build/nice_banana_toy.svg");
 std::print("Five exact banana coordinates; native analytic log densities match their inverse standard-normal coordinates.\n");
}
